<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalyan Minaret</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1127</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Minaret of Kalyan, Great Minaret</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalyan">Kalyan</a> Minaret is a brick minaret in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, built in 1127 by the Kara-Khanid ruler Arslan Khan Muhammad. About 46 metres high and decorated with bands of patterned brickwork, it was the tallest building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> for centuries. According to tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> spared it when he sacked Bukhara in 1220. It stands beside the Kalyan Mosque and the Mir-i Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">Madrasa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalyan-minaret.jpg" alt="Kalyan Minaret" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar Milošević · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalyan_Minaret,_Bukhara_(%D0%9C%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%9A%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%8F%D0%BD_%D0%B2_%D0%91%D1%83%D1%85%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B5,_Minorai_Kalon).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
